  <!-- ========== 3. 24-48H DELTA ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="delta">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      What Moved (Last 24-48h)
      <span class="meta">Descriptive &middot; banned-verb lint enforced &middot; each paragraph cites a forecast or is marked context-only</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="delta-narrative">

      <p>On October 1, President Trump publicly rejected Iran&rsquo;s seven-day ceasefire proposal, which had called for the Strait of Hormuz to reopen within six days and nuclear talks to begin on day seven. Trump told reporters: &ldquo;I reject their proposal. They want to make a deal to open the Hormuz Strait immediately because they&rsquo;re losing so badly.&rdquo; Washington subsequently transmitted a counterproposal to Tehran through Qatari and Pakistani intermediaries; Iran&rsquo;s Deputy Foreign Minister Kazem Gharibabadi confirmed receipt and stated publicly that &ldquo;the ball is in the United States&rsquo; court to choose either the path of diplomacy or continuation of a confrontational approach.&rdquo; As of October 4, Iran had not issued a formal response to the US counterproposal. US and Iranian officials are described by both sides as broadly aligned on the steps required but divided on the order &mdash; specifically, whether the nuclear program must be addressed before or after a Hormuz reopening and sanctions relief. → supports <span class="cite">F8</span> without move</p>

      <p>The United States announced on October 1 the deployment of a third aircraft carrier strike group to Central Command&rsquo;s area of operations: the USS Theodore Roosevelt, accompanied by the Makin Island Amphibious Ready Group carrying approximately 2,000 Marines. The deployment adds roughly 9,000&ndash;10,000 service personnel to the region, alongside the USS George H.W. Bush and USS George Washington already present. Trump stated publicly that if no deal with Iran is reached, strikes could resume after the November midterm elections. Senior Trump administration officials met at Camp David on October 3&ndash;4 to discuss Iran war strategy. The Theodore Roosevelt is not expected to arrive in the CENTCOM area of operations until late November. → supports <span class="cite">F9</span> without move</p>

      <p>Across the Strait of Hormuz corridor, AIS and IMF PortWatch data showed approximately one commercial transit on September 27 &mdash; against an 85-vessels-per-day pre-war baseline. As of the early October reporting period, 139 AIS-visible vessels remained holding position outside their berths in the Gulf watch box, and five vessel strikes near the Strait were reported over the week preceding October 4. The cumulative evidence confirms F4&rsquo;s resolution: the 30-vessels-per-day threshold was never approached during the full September 3 through October 3 forecast window. → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>

      <p>Israeli election campaign dynamics on October 4&ndash;5 were shaped by the imminent third anniversary of the October 7, 2023 Hamas attack, falling on October 7 &mdash; two days from this report&rsquo;s date. Eisenkot has made a central campaign message of the statement: &ldquo;For the future of Israel, we must ensure that next October, the October 7 government ends its role in history.&rdquo; Polling released through Sunday October 4 showed the race near-tied: most surveys average Yashar at 23&ndash;24 seats and Likud at 21&ndash;23, though a KAN News poll and a Channel 12 poll both reversed the average lead, placing Likud at 24 seats and Yashar at 22&ndash;23. At the bloc level, most surveys project the opposition at approximately 62 seats &mdash; above the 61-seat majority threshold &mdash; and the Netanyahu bloc at 48&ndash;55 seats. → moved <span class="cite">F5</span></p>

      <p>GlobalSecurity&rsquo;s Day 218 operations report (October 3) confirmed that no coalition strike on Iranian territory ashore had been recorded for 26 consecutive operational periods, and no Iranian attack on a host state for 24 consecutive days. Iranian Foreign Minister Araghchi stated publicly that &ldquo;there will be no military solution&rdquo; to the conflict; IRGC-affiliated chief negotiator Ghalibaf countered that &ldquo;the era of proportionate responses is over.&rdquo; The operational lull runs concurrent with Trump&rsquo;s domestic political calendar ahead of November 4 US midterms, with no CENTCOM press release confirming offensive strikes on Iran in the relevant period. → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>

    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ========== 4. SCENARIO MAP ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="scenario-map">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      Scenario Map
      <span class="meta">Computed from Forecast Board &middot; 1 sentence per scenario</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="scenario-map">
      <div class="prob-bar-container">
        <div class="prob-bar prob-escalation" style="width:30%;">Escalation 30%</div>
        <div class="prob-bar prob-status" style="width:55%;">Protracted 55%</div>
        <div class="prob-bar prob-deescalation" style="width:15%;">De-esc 15%</div>
      </div>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-esc">Escalation path (30%):</strong> Trump follows through on post-midterm strike threat if Iran rejects or stalls on the US counterproposal, with the Theodore Roosevelt CSG potentially accelerating timeline if it arrives early in the CENTCOM AOR.</p>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-prot">Protracted path (55%):</strong> The current operational lull persists through and past midterms &mdash; neither a breakthrough deal nor a major escalation &mdash; as both sides exchange counterproposals while nuclear sequencing remains unresolved and Hormuz stays effectively closed.</p>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-desc">De-escalation path (15%):</strong> Iran accepts the US counterproposal before Oct 27, producing a written framework that enables a partial Hormuz reopening and suspends active hostilities pending further nuclear talks.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ========== 5. INSIDE IRAN ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="inside-iran">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      Inside Iran
      <span class="meta">State-media decode &middot; ~200 words &middot; verb-ban + forecast-cite rule applies</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="inside-iran">
      <div class="iran-media-badges">
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRNA</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRIB</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Press TV</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Fars</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Tasnim</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Kayhan</span>
      </div>

      <p>Iranian state media &mdash; IRNA, Press TV, and Tasnim &mdash; framed Trump&rsquo;s October 1 rejection of the seven-day Hormuz proposal not as an Iranian diplomatic setback but as evidence of American electoral self-interest. IRNA&rsquo;s lead coverage carried Gharibabadi&rsquo;s statement under headlines emphasizing Iran&rsquo;s role as seeking &ldquo;a just and permanent peace,&rdquo; and state television broadcast analysis asserting that Trump&rsquo;s rejection was &ldquo;driven by domestic electoral calculations ahead of November midterms&rdquo; rather than substantive objections to Iran&rsquo;s terms. State media gave no acknowledgment that Iran&rsquo;s own sequencing demands &mdash; requiring sanctions relief, port blockade removal, and ceasefire guarantees before nuclear talks begin &mdash; were the sticking point identified by US officials in the same period. → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>

      <p>On the military dimension, Tasnim News Agency ran analysis citing unnamed IRGC commanders characterizing the US third-carrier deployment as &ldquo;a desperate escalation designed to intimidate the Islamic Republic,&rdquo; and asserting that US carrier groups &ldquo;remain within range of our precision missiles.&rdquo; Press TV framed the 26-day lull in coalition strikes on Iranian territory as evidence that &ldquo;the enemy has recognized its inability to achieve military objectives.&rdquo; State media avoided any reference to the 139-vessel backlog accumulating outside the Strait, the five vessel strikes in one week, or the toll of the Hormuz closure on Iranian petrochemical exports &mdash; instead characterizing the Strait interdiction as Iran&rsquo;s &ldquo;legitimate defensive right under international law.&rdquo; → supports <span class="cite">F9</span> without move</p>

    </div>
  </div>

